{
 "translation": "If he asked the reason, she would say nothing but \"Ooh-hoo\" and the tired Sundarlal would doze off again. However, once in the very beginning, Sundarlal had only asked this much about Lajwanti's dark days: \"Who was he?\" Lajwanti lowered her gaze and said, \"Jamal.\" Then she wanted to say something, fixing her gaze on Sundarlal's face, but Sundarlal was looking at Lajwanti's face with a strange look and stroking her hair. Lajwanti again lowered her eyes, and Sundarlal asked, \"Did he treat you well?\" \"Yes.\" \"He didn't hit you, did he?\" Lajwanti, sliding her head onto Sundarlal's chest, said, \"No.\" And then she spoke, \"He didn't hit me, but I was more afraid of him. You used to hit me too, but I wasn't afraid of you. Now you won't hit me, will you?\" Tears welled up in Sundarlal's eyes, and he said with great regret and remorse, \"No, Devi.\" “No, no, I won't hit you,” Sundarlal said. Lajwanti thought, and she too began to cry. And after that, Lajwanti wanted to tell everything, but Sundarlal said, “Let bygones be bygones. What fault is yours in this? The fault lies with our society, which does not give respect to goddesses like you in its midst. It does not harm you, it harms itself.” And Lajwanti's thoughts remained in her mind. She could not say everything and remained quiet and subdued, looking at her body, which after the partition had now become the body of a goddess, not Lajwanti's. She was happy, very happy, but immersed in such happiness that contained doubt and apprehension. She would suddenly sit up while lying down, as if, in moments of extreme happiness, hearing a sound, she would suddenly become attentive to it. When many days passed, doubt completely took the place of happiness. Not because Sundarlal Babu had started his old mistreatment again, but because he had started treating Lajo very well. Such treatment, which Lajo did not expect. She wanted to be the old Lajo who would fight over a carrot and be appeased by a radish. But now there was no question of a fight. Sunderlal had made her feel as if she was a glass object that would break with a touch. And Lajo would look at her reflection in the mirror and finally come to the conclusion that she could be everything else, but not Lajo. She was settled but ruined. Sunderlal had no eyes to see her tears, nor ears to hear her sighs. Morning processions kept coming out, and the neighborhood Mullah Shakoor, along with reformer Rasalu and Nekiram, kept singing in the same voice: 'The hands that brought the plants have withered, Lajo, the plants.'",
